      "paragraphs": {
        "paragraphs": [
          {
            "index": 0,
            "blockIndex": 0,
            "paragraphInBlock": 0,
            "text": "A sounding object can vibrate freely.\nA periodic external force can also drive its motion.\nNear its natural frequency, the driven response can grow greatly: this is resonance."
          },
          {
            "index": 1,
            "blockIndex": 1,
            "paragraphInBlock": 0,
            "text": "Two identical tuning forks demonstrate **sound resonance**.\nSet them a short distance apart on wooden resonance boxes.\nLet the box openings face each other (Figure 83)."
          },
          {
            "index": 2,
            "blockIndex": 1,
            "paragraphInBlock": 1,
            "text": "Tap one arm of one fork with a small hammer.\nThe other fork soon begins to sound as well.\nSound waves from the first apply a periodic force to it."
          },
          {
            "index": 3,
            "blockIndex": 1,
            "paragraphInBlock": 2,
            "text": "Their matching natural frequencies allow a large response.\nDifferent natural frequencies give a much weaker response.\nThe other fork then usually makes no clearly audible sound."
          },
          {
            "index": 4,
            "blockIndex": 1,
            "paragraphInBlock": 3,
            "text": "Attach a light metal clip to either identical fork.\nThe clip changes that fork's natural frequency.\nThe original resonance weakens or is no longer audible."
          },
          {
            "index": 5,
            "blockIndex": 4,
            "paragraphInBlock": 0,
            "text": "Tuning forks have little damping, so their resonance curve is sharp.\nThis is the behavior discussed in Section 14."
          }
        ],
